WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.960
Whether they feature lo-fi&nbsp;
samurai or jazzy bounty hunters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.423
these are the best anime openings that&nbsp;
you won't be skipping any time soon.

00:00:08.538 --> 00:00:13.020
The best openings don't always come from&nbsp;
the best anime. Is "Lucky Star" a series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:18.060
that stands the test of time? Not really. The&nbsp;
humor is a mix of dry slice-of-life material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:23.100
that other shows have done better, with plenty&nbsp;
of otaku culture references that instantly date&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:28.440
the series to 2007. Older fans seeking a&nbsp;
nostalgic flashback to a previous era of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:33.000
anime might find some enjoyment in revisiting the&nbsp;
series, but the majority of the fun is contained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:38.100
in the opening theme "Motteke! Sailor Fuku,"&nbsp;
which translates to "Take It! Sailor Clothes."

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.880
The majority of "Lucky Star" is very&nbsp;
slow-paced, but the opening sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:46.080
is pure hyperactive energy. The lyrics,&nbsp;
consisting mostly of nonsense about school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.840
uniforms performed by voice actors Aya&nbsp;
Hirano, Emiri Katou, Kaori Fukuhara, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.460
Aya Endo. The dancing takes after the ending&nbsp;
theme to Kyoto Animation's previous hit "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:02.064
Melancholy of Haruhi Suzumiya," and makes&nbsp;
for a super entertaining bit of animation.

00:01:02.219 --> 00:01:07.140
"Just Between Us" by Chatmonchy is a cute and&nbsp;
fitting theme song for "Princess Jellyfish,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:12.180
a dramedy about a jellyfish-obsessed woman who&nbsp;
befriends a beautiful crossdresser and has to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:16.920
her man-fearing roommates from finding out. What&nbsp;
really makes this opening sequence special and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:21.900
worthy of inclusion on this list isn't so much the&nbsp;
song itself, but the visuals that accompany it.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:26.520
In just 90 seconds, this opening fits in&nbsp;
references to "Sex and the City," "Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.760
Wars," "Singin' in the Rain," "Mary&nbsp;
Poppins," "The Graduate," James Bond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:34.080
and even Bruce Lee. The first time you see it,&nbsp;
it's the perfect bizarre hook for the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.320
and once you start watching, you&nbsp;
come to appreciate how well the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.524
smaller details in all these parodies&nbsp;
fit each character's personalities.

00:01:41.834 --> 00:01:45.540
"Pop Team Epic" is one of the&nbsp;
strangest anime comedies around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:49.620
a collection of short mixed-media skits&nbsp;
that feature multiple voice actors taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:54.600
the roles of the chaotic schoolgirls&nbsp;
Popuko and Pipimi. The opening theme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.600 --> 00:02:00.420
a techno track also titled "Pop Team Epic"&nbsp;
performed by Sumire Uesaka, is a bomb of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:05.160
pure sensory overload that manages to perfectly&nbsp;
encapsulate the chaos that the show is known for.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:10.080
Starting with a nail-covered baseball bat&nbsp;
destroying a TV set, the images that make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.700
up this high-octane intro are as hilarious as&nbsp;
they are fleeting. These girls are everywhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.600
showing up in random places across the&nbsp;
globe, spying on you in different forms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:22.920
and traveling through time to remake the&nbsp;
world. Why do they exist? What do they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:27.301
want with us? No one knows for sure…&nbsp;
but we should probably be very afraid.

00:02:27.594 --> 00:02:29.718
"Your life has been spared!"

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:35.580
Before lofi hip hop became inextricably linked&nbsp;
to anime girls studying, there was "Samurai&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:41.040
Champloo." This 2004 anime about two rival&nbsp;
ronin working together to help a girl find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.480
"the samurai who smells of sunflowers"&nbsp;
brought hip hop music and attitude into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:51.300
Edo period Japan. Director Shinichiro Watanabe&nbsp;
is perhaps the best anime director around when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:56.160
it comes to effectively using music, and his&nbsp;
anime always have outstanding opening themes.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:01.260
The "Samurai Champloo" theme song "Battlecry"&nbsp;
was composed by the late, great Nujabes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:06.300
with lyrics written and performed by Shing02.&nbsp;
The mix of contemporary musical styles with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:10.680
traditional Japanese art, and of chill beats&nbsp;
with intense lyrics and violent action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:15.000
provide a striking first impression for&nbsp;
anyone not yet familiar with the show.

00:03:15.338 --> 00:03:20.400
A number of anime have made effective use&nbsp;
of Western pop and rock songs for openings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.300
over the years, including Duran Duran's&nbsp;
"Girls on Film" in "Speed Grapher," The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:28.920
Delgados' "The Light Before We Land" in&nbsp;
"Gunslinger Girl," and Oasis' "Falling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:32.220
Down" in "Eden of the East." One of the&nbsp;
most famous examples of this, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:38.040
is the use of "Duvet" by Bôa in the 1998&nbsp;
cyberpunk classic "Serial Experiments Lain."

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:41.700
"Serial Experiments Lain," a show&nbsp;
about a teenage girl drawn into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:46.440
web of online conspiracies, is at once&nbsp;
artsy, complex, scary, depressing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:50.820
and yet also beautiful — all features that&nbsp;
the opening theme exemplifies. The credits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:55.200
open with an ominous narrator letting you know&nbsp;
exactly when this particular series takes place.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:04:02.880
"Present day… present time! Hahahahaha—"

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:07.260
The bizarre message is followed up by the&nbsp;
smooth sounds of "Duvet" and a showcase of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:11.760
anime's striking visuals. "Lain" was certainly&nbsp;
prophetic about the dangers of the Metaverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.401
but the internet today doesn't look&nbsp;
anywhere near as cool as it did here.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.620
A list of the best anime opening themes&nbsp;
wouldn't be complete without at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:24.780
one upbeat shonen opening. Long-running battle&nbsp;
shonen tend to cycle through a lot of openings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:27.660
so options for this category&nbsp;
were numerous. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:32.280
ranking the best "Naruto" or "One Piece"&nbsp;
themes could be full lists unto themselves.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:37.080
"Yu Yu Hakusho" has a somewhat unusual&nbsp;
advantage, in that it used the super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:43.440
catchy song "Hohoemi no Bakudan" — or "Smile&nbsp;
Bomb" — for the entirety of its 112-episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:50.340
run from 1992 to 1995. Changes were made to the&nbsp;
opening animation in Episode 67 and Episode 94,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:52.860
but the iconic tune has always remained the same.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:57.300
"Smile Bomb" was performed in&nbsp;
Japanese by Matsuko Mawatari. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:02.040
"Yu Yu Hakusho" was among the first anime&nbsp;
dubbed by Funimation, which used to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:06.240
English language translations of the opening&nbsp;
music to accompany their dubs. Thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:11.262
the English version of "Smile Bomb" by Sara White&nbsp;
manages to be just as good as the Japanese one.

00:05:11.397 --> 00:05:16.560
Some anime purists might consider it sacrilege&nbsp;
to list an American opening theme on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:21.360
list — much less one from 4Kids Entertainment,&nbsp;
the infamous former dubbing studio that gave us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:26.520
the horrific "One Piece" rap among other crimes&nbsp;
against music. But every rule has an exception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:31.560
and the English opening to the first season of&nbsp;
"Pokemon," performed by Jason Paige, may be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:36.300
single localized opening theme that is actually&nbsp;
an improvement over the original Japanese one.

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:40.920
If you were young — or young at heart — when&nbsp;
"Pokemon" hit the airwaves in the late-90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:46.080
you can no doubt recite the lyrics to the opening&nbsp;
theme song by heart. Start singing "I wanna be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:50.760
very best" at an anime convention, and you can&nbsp;
guarantee that others will join in with "like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:55.620
no one ever was." If you need more evidence of&nbsp;
this infectiously catchy song's superiority,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.620 --> 00:06:00.180
keep in mind that when "Pokemon" was exported&nbsp;
globally, other countries didn't adapt the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:04.980
Japanese theme or make their own openings — they&nbsp;
dubbed the English theme. It's just that powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:09.734
around the world, with the ability to keep us&nbsp;
motivated in even the most difficult times.

00:06:09.734 --> 00:06:15.322
"It's you and me… I know&nbsp;
it's my destiny, Pokemon!"

00:06:15.878 --> 00:06:21.600
Every season of "Mob Psycho 100" thus far has had&nbsp;
incredible opening themes, each performed by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:26.820
series' own musical group, Mob Choir. The first&nbsp;
opening, titled "99," arguably deserves to be on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:31.560
this list for its beautiful animation, inspiring&nbsp;
lyrics, and the intense countdown ratcheting up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:36.300
the anxiety. "1," the third season's opening, was&nbsp;
released months in advance of the season premiere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:39.960
and proved to be another masterpiece even&nbsp;
without the context of the new episodes.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:45.300
If we had to choose, however, "99.9" — the&nbsp;
second season's opening featuring the band&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:49.860
Sajou no Hana alongside the Mob Choir —&nbsp;
is the best possible choice to represent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:53.820
"Mob Psycho 100." "99" was always&nbsp;
going to be a hard opening to top,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:58.320
but "99.9" went above and beyond with&nbsp;
even more exciting music and trippy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:02.580
animation. It won best opening sequence&nbsp;
at the 2020 Crunchyroll Anime Awards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:07.603
and some critics and fans have gone so far as to&nbsp;
declare it the best anime opening of all time.

00:07:07.718 --> 00:07:12.720
Gentleman thief Lupin the 3rd could probably&nbsp;
be considered the anime world's equivalent of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:16.920
James Bond. As such, it's only fitting that&nbsp;
his theme music is as cool and instantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:21.720
memorable as 007's — even if he didn't receive&nbsp;
it in his first appearance. Directed by future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:27.240
Studio Ghibli co-founders Hayao Miyazaki&nbsp;
and Isao Takahata, "Lupin the 3rd Part I"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:31.320
was something of a mixed bag. The first half&nbsp;
of the series is dark and gritty in places,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:35.460
while the second half is more family-friendly.&nbsp;
The creators were still figuring out what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:39.642
worked for the character, and its repetitive&nbsp;
theme is definitely not one of the greats.

00:07:39.642 --> 00:07:43.001
"Opera fan, probably." "Everybody's a critic."

00:07:43.001 --> 00:07:46.500
"Lupin the 3rd Part III" went in&nbsp;
a different, wackier direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:51.960
and its opening reflects that. However, it was&nbsp;
the theme for "Lupin the 3rd Part II" that became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:55.980
the true "Lupin" theme. Remixed multiple&nbsp;
times over the course of the series' run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.980 --> 00:08:00.420
it set the standard for jazzy adventure anime&nbsp;
themes and has influenced countless anime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:04.740
openings. In the 21st century, "Part&nbsp;
IV," "Part V," and "Part VI" have all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:09.563
returned to the "Part II" theme while adding&nbsp;
their own great animated sequences on top.

00:08:09.734 --> 00:08:15.180
Satoshi Kon didn't make anime the way anyone&nbsp;
else did, and the opening sequence to "Paranoia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:21.180
Agent" — the one TV series he directed before&nbsp;
his death in 2010 — is unlike any other. "Yume no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:27.180
Shima Shinen Kouen" — or "Dream Island Obsessional&nbsp;
Park" — by composer Susumu Hirasawa was designed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:32.040
to instantly wake up viewers tuning into this&nbsp;
2004 late-night psychological horror series.

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:37.080
"Paranoia Agent" is all about the allures and&nbsp;
dangers of escaping from the difficulties of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:41.940
reality, and the opening sequence illustrates this&nbsp;
theme in the eeriest way possible. The show's cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:46.260
of characters are shown drowning underwater,&nbsp;
falling from the sky, and standing in the middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:51.360
of traffic, all while laughing hysterically.&nbsp;
The bombastic electronic music is overpowering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:55.740
in a way that feels like yet another level of&nbsp;
disassociation, with lyrics about beautiful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.740 --> 00:09:01.064
mushroom clouds and not worrying about tsunamis&nbsp;
contributing to the surreal sense of disaster.

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:06.840
Can a great opening alone turn an anime into&nbsp;
a phenomenon? The TV adaptation of Hajime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:10.980
Isayama's "Attack on Titan" was going&nbsp;
to be a blockbuster hit no matter what,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:14.460
but the effectiveness of its first&nbsp;
opening certainly helped its success.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:19.560
Linked Horizon's "Feuerroter Pfeil und&nbsp;
Bogen" — from the German for "Crimson Bow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:24.180
and Arrow" — sold skeptics on this particular&nbsp;
show potentially being the coolest thing ever.

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:28.920
The plot of "Attack on Titan" turned messy and&nbsp;
controversial, but even if you hate the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:33.420
itself, this opening is still perfectly calibrated&nbsp;
to get your blood pumping and make you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:38.400
to fight some Titans yourself. The show has had&nbsp;
similarly great openings in its later seasons, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:43.563
none can match the first one — every action-packed&nbsp;
second of this theme became instantly iconic.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:48.600
The figure skating romance "Yuri on Ice"&nbsp;
became an instant phenomenon among anime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:53.160
fans in 2016. It ended up sweeping the&nbsp;
first ever Crunchyroll Anime Awards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:57.900
winning all seven categories it was nominated&nbsp;
in. While there was some controversy about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:01.740
show winning so many awards against some&nbsp;
worthy competitors, it's still undoubtedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:06.900
one of the best anime of the past decade.&nbsp;
Regardless, "History Maker" by Dean Fujioka&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:11.842
was a genuinely deserving winner for best opening&nbsp;
and deserves the number four spot on this list.

00:10:11.842 --> 00:10:16.702
"Well, this won't be an official record, but&nbsp;
that score's among the top ten in the world!"

00:10:16.702 --> 00:10:21.480
Performed in English, the lyrics to "History&nbsp;
Maker" are all sung by Fujioka, but are written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:26.700
essentially as a duet. The first verse is from the&nbsp;
perspective of the anxiety-ridden Yuri, while the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:31.200
second is from the point of view of his supportive&nbsp;
coach Victor. The triumphant chorus is meant to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:35.760
the two of them together. The beautiful music is&nbsp;
matched with equally beautiful animation, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:40.784
stylized and expressive renderings of the main&nbsp;
characters skating their way around the screen.

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:45.000
As the "Lucky Star" girls proved, having&nbsp;
anime characters dance to their own theme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:49.200
songs can be an easy formula for a winning&nbsp;
introduction. "Keep Your Hands Off Eizouken!"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:53.160
provides another dance-heavy opening,&nbsp;
set to the upbeat anthem "Easy Breezy"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:57.360
by the rap duo Chelmico. It stands out&nbsp;
both due to the sheer catchiness of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:01.440
music and the personality the artists&nbsp;
at Science SARU put into the animation.

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:05.520
"Keep Your Hands Off Eizouken!" is about&nbsp;
high school girls making their own anime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:10.140
Unlike most other high school anime, where&nbsp;
characters tend to be at least drawn if not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:14.880
written as idealized archetypes, the "Eizouken"&nbsp;
girls are designed to be as quirky as their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:19.560
personalities. This definitely comes across&nbsp;
in the opening: Asakusa's awkward charm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:24.900
Kanamori's cynical confidence, and Mizusaki's&nbsp;
freewheeling joy can be seen directly in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.900 --> 00:11:29.520
moves. The second half of the opening moves&nbsp;
on from the dancing to a fast-paced barrage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:35.084
of iconography associated with the characters,&nbsp;
building to an incredibly exciting title drop.

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:39.360
If this list were specifically ranking&nbsp;
the most influential anime openings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:44.040
then "A Cruel Angel's Thesis" from "Neon&nbsp;
Genesis Evangelion" would probably clinch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:48.960
the top spot. Performed by Yoko Takahashi,&nbsp;
the song is sung from the perspective of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:53.100
maternal figure inspiring a young boy to&nbsp;
"become a legend." An uplifting contrast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:57.600
to a show that would soon prove itself to be&nbsp;
extremely dark and heavy, "A Cruel Angel's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:02.460
Thesis" exploded in popularity even beyond the&nbsp;
massive success of "Evangelion" itself — it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:07.020
was the single most popular karaoke song of&nbsp;
Japan's Heisei era, according to Crunchyroll.

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:12.000
The opening sequence's visuals have proven&nbsp;
just as iconic as the music. "Evangelion"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:15.120
might have gone through some significant&nbsp;
changes over the course of its production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:20.520
but the opening's rapid montage of images, title&nbsp;
cards, and symbolism is packed with foreshadowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:24.900
that pays off at the end of the series. It's&nbsp;
probably the ideal of what people think of as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.900 --> 00:12:29.523
an "anime opening," and it's been lovingly&nbsp;
parodied on numerous occasions as a result.

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:35.460
Three, two, one, let's jam. Yoko Kanno is perhaps&nbsp;
the greatest composer to have ever worked in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:40.020
anime — her themes for "Vision of Escaflowne"&nbsp;
and "Wolf's Rain" were both under consideration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:44.400
for this list. What was never in question,&nbsp;
however, was that "Tank!," the iconic opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:49.740
to Shinichiro Watanabe's beloved sci-fi Western&nbsp;
"Cowboy Bebop," was certain to be number one.

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:54.780
Though "Tank!" and "Cowboy Bebop" as a whole took&nbsp;
clear inspiration from "Lupin III," they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:59.640
always stood out from the general popular trends&nbsp;
in anime. The incredible big band jazz tune makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:04.901
for one of the coolest sequences in anime history&nbsp;
— even if Jet isn't the biggest fan of the genre.

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:06.560
"I thought you like jazz.”

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:10.200
"Don't be dense. I started&nbsp;
wailing the blues when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:12.821
doctor whacked my bottom on the day I was born."

00:13:12.821 --> 00:13:16.320
This highly influential opening&nbsp;
theme was many years in the making,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:20.820
as Kanno explained during an interview with&nbsp;
Red Bull Music Academy. She elaborated —

00:13:20.820 --> 00:13:24.600
"The seeds for that score were sown in middle&nbsp;
school and high school when I was a member of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:28.980
the brass band [...] I wanted to play brass&nbsp;
music that shook your soul, made your blood boil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:33.240
and made you lose it. [...] I wanted to make music&nbsp;
which would light a fire in me when I played it."

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:36.720
That fire caught hold of countless&nbsp;
anime fans across the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:39.242
and it still burns brightly to this day.

